{"causes": ["The War of 1812 created an urgent need for American naval dominance on the Great Lakes to secure the nation's northern frontier.", "The strategic importance of controlling Lake Erie for both military logistics and territorial claims prompted the United States to build a fleet there.", "Perry's familial background within a prominent naval dynasty provided him with early exposure and opportunities for command in the burgeoning U.S. Navy."], "effects": ["Perry's victory at Lake Erie secured American control over the Great Lakes, thereby preventing a British invasion from the north.", "The defeat of the British fleet enabled William Henry Harrison's subsequent victory at the Battle of the Thames and the collapse of Tecumseh's Confederacy.", "Perry's iconic phrase, 'We have met the enemy and they are ours,' became a enduring symbol of American naval prowess and national pride."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "war-of-1812", "sourceName": "War of 1812", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "oliver-hazard-perry", "targetName": "Oliver Hazard Perry", "context": "Oliver Hazard Perry achieved his most significant military successes during the War of 1812, particularly the Battle of Lake Erie in 1813."}, {"sourceSlug": "oliver-hazard-perry", "sourceName": "Oliver Hazard Perry", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-lake-erie", "targetName": "Battle of Lake Erie", "context": "Oliver Hazard Perry commanded the American forces to a decisive victory over the British fleet at the Battle of Lake Erie on September 10, 1813."}, {"sourceSlug": "oliver-hazard-perry", "sourceName": "Oliver Hazard Perry", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "william-henry-harrison", "targetName": "William Henry Harrison", "context": "Perry's victory on Lake Erie secured supply lines and enabled William Henry Harrison's subsequent land victory at the Battle of the Thames in October 1813."}, {"sourceSlug": "oliver-hazard-perry", "sourceName": "Oliver Hazard Perry", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "great-lakes", "targetName": "Great Lakes", "context": "Oliver Hazard Perry's most significant naval campaigns and his decisive victory took place in the Great Lakes region of North America."}, {"sourceSlug": "matthew-c-perry", "sourceName": "Matthew C. Perry", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "oliver-hazard-perry", "targetName": "Oliver Hazard Perry", "context": "Oliver Hazard Perry, as the elder brother, likely influenced the early naval career and aspirations of his younger brother, Matthew C. Perry, who also became a prominent commodore."}], "places": [{"name": "South Kingstown, United States", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Lake Erie, North America", "role": "Major battle site"}, {"name": "Port-of-Spain, Trinidad and Tobago", "role": "Place of death"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Oliver Hazard Perry", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "oliver-hazard-perry"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q580513", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oliver_Hazard_Perry"], "tags": ["United States", "Military Leaders & Commanders", "Americas", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "empire-and-colonialism"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Oliver Hazard Perry's decisive victory at the Battle of Lake Erie shifted the strategic balance of the War of 1812, directly leading to the collapse of Native American resistance in the Northwest and securing American control over a vital frontier. His actions had a lasting impact on U.S. territorial integrity and national identity.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-05T14:22:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37323797079", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Oliver Hazard Perry was a United States Navy officer from South Kingstown, Rhode Island. A prominent member of the Perry family naval dynasty, he was the son of Sarah Wallace Alexander and Captain Christopher Raymond Perry, and older brother of Commodore Matthew C. Perry.", "newValue": "Oliver Hazard Perry (1785–1819) was a pivotal United States Navy commodore whose decisive victory at the Battle of Lake Erie in 1813 fundamentally altered the strategic landscape of the War of 1812, securing American control over the Great Lakes and significantly weakening British and Native American alliances in the Northwest Territory. Born into a distinguished naval family in South Kingstown, Rhode Island, Perry's early career was shaped by the burgeoning American maritime power and the geopolitical tensions of the early 19th century, marking him as a key figure in the nation's struggle for sovereignty and expansion.

Perry's most celebrated achievement occurred on September 10, 1813, when, commanding a hastily constructed fleet of nine ships against a more experienced British squadron under Robert Heriot Barclay, he engaged in a brutal three-hour battle near Put-in-Bay, Ohio. After his flagship, USS Lawrence, was disabled, Perry famously transferred his command to the USS Niagara and pressed the attack, ultimately forcing the surrender of the entire British fleet. His dispatch, 'We have met the enemy and they are ours,' became an iconic declaration of American naval prowess and a rallying cry for the war effort, directly leading to William Henry Harrison's victory at the Battle of the Thames.

The strategic triumph on Lake Erie had profound and immediate consequences, forcing the British to retreat from Detroit and enabling American forces to reclaim lost territory in the Northwest. It severely disrupted British supply lines and isolated their Native American allies, most notably Tecumseh's confederacy, which suffered a critical blow with Tecumseh's death shortly thereafter. Perry's victory solidified American claims to the Great Lakes frontier, influencing subsequent border negotiations and contributing to the eventual expansion of the United States westward."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-05T14:22:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37323797079", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1785-08-23, South Kingstown"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1785-08-23, South Kingstown"}], "newValue": ["The War of 1812 created an urgent need for American naval dominance on the Great Lakes to secure the nation's northern frontier.", "The strategic importance of controlling Lake Erie for both military logistics and territorial claims prompted the United States to build a fleet there.", "Perry's familial background within a prominent naval dynasty provided him with early exposure and opportunities for command in the burgeoning U.S. Navy."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-05T14:22:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37323797079", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1819-08-23"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1819-08-23"}], "newValue": ["Perry's victory at Lake Erie secured American control over the Great Lakes, thereby preventing a British invasion from the north.", "The de